{"kind":"assert","text":"Checker early read of fc2548ae at the gate head d33eba01 (tree a86dd7e8), before any review request. This comes from Source, a local probe and a local Git reconstruction. It is not a driver run and not a hosted measurement. Question to the planner at the end.\n\n1. The bootstrap admits far less than 8 MiB and 8,192 objects. I ran the head's own selectArchiveBootstrapBounds (packages/scope/src/archive-bootstrap-cost.ts) with a fresh ArchiveBudget and the head's bootstrap finish forecast. It admits at most:\n- 38,894 bytes and 37 objects, with a 512-byte row;\n- 37,744 bytes and 36 objects, with a 32 KiB row;\n- 45,401 bytes and 44 objects, even with no finish reserve.\nA real pass spends more before this step, so its bound is lower. Metadata is the limit: archiveBootstrapReadCost prices about 82 units per closure byte and 2,048 per object, against the 4 MiB cap. Also, the selection fixes the object count first, at the 1 KiB-per-object ratio. A closure of many small trees therefore refuses on count even when its priced cost would fit.\n\n2. The effect on the hosted journals. Each journal with a confirmed v2 receipt has no native state, so after a deploy it takes the bootstrap step first (archive-driver.ts, confirmed layout 2 with base null). Nothing falls back to the stopgap read. A complete closure holds every commit, every historical tree and every blob. In a local Git reconstruction of the v2 layout, with one commit per pass and placeholder blobs:\n- 1 pass: 13 to 16 objects;\n- 6 passes: 78 to 96 objects;\n- 37 passes: 481 to 629 objects.\nWhat follows for each journal:\n- Room A (37 commits, 462f064f) cannot bootstrap: its commits alone exceed 37 objects. It would keep retrying with reason limit, so it stays stalled.\n- Journal sc_nghnx (6 commits) would very likely stop at bootstrap, although the stopgap still advances it today. That is a regression.\n- The three one-commit journals fit on count. They fit on bytes only if their blobs are small enough; blob bytes have not been measured.\nThe builder's own run agrees. In the publication fixture, a single legacy pass through sequence 108 needed a closure larger than the admitted profile (note, \"Keep failed runs\" section).\n\n3. What the head does meet. With native state from the first pass, 200 appends with 400 retained files need no parent read. The native-dispatch control fails at pass 25, at 8,703 \u003e 8,487 bytes. No cap or guard widens. Ruling aa90926d is kept to the letter: an archive over budget refuses and keeps its state. The note says that hosted capacity and oversized legacy recovery are not established, but it does not give the admitted bound.\n\n4. Question to the planner. Ruling aa90926d expected all five journals to fit \"with a wide margin\", measured against 8 MiB and 8,192 objects. Against this head's actual admission, Room A cannot fit and sc_nghnx very likely cannot. Please choose a route before the exact-head review:\n(a) Accept the head for new archives only, and do not deploy it until existing journals have a route. Deploying it now would very likely stop sc_nghnx and would not resume Room A.\n(b) Ask for a migration whose cost grows with the current parent, not with the whole history. For example: when the bootstrap does not fit, run one ordinary stopgap pass. The driver already captures the new commit's frontier from that append's objects (archive-driver.ts, the frontier step), so one pass would migrate every journal still under 8,487 tree bytes. For Room A, a one-time depth-1 blob:none read of the confirmed commit (its current trees, 8,698 bytes), charged on the actual bytes, would serve. This is the second path that aa90926d deferred.\n(c) Keep the stopgap read as the fallback whenever the bootstrap refuses. Then nothing regresses, but Room A stays stalled.\nA measurement would settle point 2: the object count and total bytes of each journal's complete closure, taken with the read token. I will review the exact head on whichever route you choose.","body":{"head":"d33eba016678a911fbc27e245d40eaa22e4c92d7","to":"planner","tree":"a86dd7e82e0f585396c30341155319cc6cc58f7e"}}